extraction_notes: "Rifts World Book 18: Mystic Russia pp.148-152. THIS CLASS DRAWS ON THREE OF THE FOUR TRADITIONS IMPORTED IN BATCH 1 - Nature, Bone and Living Fire - which is the strongest confirmation yet that the D1 prefixes work: it grants `Nature: Glimpse of the Future`, `Bone: Object Read the Dead`, `Bone: Divining Tombs & Graves`, `Nature: Negate Spoiling Magic`, `Living Fire: Bonfire of Purification` and `Living Fire: Locate & Identify Fire` by name. AND IT FOUND A REAL ERROR IN THAT IMPORT. `Nature: Glimpse of the Future` did not resolve, because batch 1d had stored the spell under its SUBTITLE - printed 132 sets ''Glimpse of the Future'' above ''A Wood & Water Divination'' above `Range:`, and a reader that walks back from `Range:` takes the nearer line. Corrected by a companion script, with the book''s own spell index at printed 6-7 as the authority; the mechanics were right and only the name was wrong. A class that cites a spell is a check on the spell import, which is why the survey orders spells before classes. The same index also shows four Nature spells the batch never had - Speed of the Snail, Summon Fog, Calm Storms and Summon Rain - which are ONE-LINE CROSS-REFERENCES with no stat block (''Identical to the spell found on page 186 of Rifts''), invisible to a reader keyed on `Range:`. Three already exist in this catalog at exactly the stated level and cost and the fourth belongs to the Rifts RPG, so none needs a row. The book prints \"Card Sharp''(+12%)\" with a stray apostrophe; the row is Cardsharp. The level-up schedule again needs two entries per level for eight consecutive levels, because each grants a named spell AND offers a pick. `secondary_skills.count` is 0: none at level one."
